PRIVATE REFLECTION: {Majority (8/12 colleagues) converged on B as the only option that (a) preserves exact bead chemistry/poly(dT) + UMI structure, (b) keeps 75×225 sequencing, and (c) solves the distance problem by converting to 5′-end capture via TSO addition after UMI, allowing template-switching during RT so CDR3 lands in the 225 bp read. Key supporting claims: standard 3′-biased capture lands in constant region; CDR3 is hundreds of bases upstream; BD’s own TCR kit uses analogous TSO/5′-RACE chemistry.  
C (favored by 4) was rejected because designing fixed capture oligos to “flank” the hypervariable CDR3 is unreliable and would still require bead redesign; A/D suffer similar hybridization or post-capture timing flaws; E violates the fixed sequencing constraint.  
No factual errors noted; minor wording differences (e.g., exact TSO placement) do not change the logic. Persistent open question: whether any lab-specific validation data exist for TSO addition on BD Rhapsody beads. Current belief: answer is B.}

